# RaySpec — environment template.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in real values. `.env` is gitignored and must
# NEVER be committed. Every value here is a placeholder — replace them.
#
# Only a subset is required to boot: a database URL, the two auth secrets, and at
# least one agent backend credential for whichever backend your spec uses. The
# rest are optional and only needed when the deployed spec declares the matching
# capability (audio/streams, playback, cron, erasure, ...).

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Database (Postgres)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Main application database. Required. The bundled dev container (`pnpm db:up`)
# listens on port 5433; point this at your own Postgres in production.
# Also available as DATABASE_URL_FILE — see the file-mount section below.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://rayspec:rayspec@localhost:5433/rayspec

# Throwaway shadow database used only for migration dry-runs. Required for the
# migration tooling; safe to point at an empty scratch database.
SHADOW_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://rayspec:rayspec@localhost:5433/rayspec_shadow

# Optional. The DBOS durable-worker SYSTEM database — a SEPARATE database from the
# app DB. Only used when the deployed spec enables the durable worker. If unset it
# is derived from DATABASE_URL by swapping the database name. Never point it at the
# app database.
# DBOS_SYSTEM_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://rayspec:rayspec@localhost:5433/rayspec_dbos_sys

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Auth secrets (required at boot — the server fails closed without them)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# HMAC pepper for API-key hashing. Use a long random per-environment value.
# Losing/rotating it invalidates every issued API key.
RAYSPEC_API_KEY_PEPPER=change-me-long-random-value

# RS256 JWT + OIDC signing key: a PKCS#8 PEM private key. The server refuses to
# boot without it. Generate a development key with:
#   openssl genpkey -algorithm RSA -pkeyopt rsa_keygen_bits:2048
# Paste the resulting PEM here (newline-escaped, single line). NEVER commit a real
# key; generate a fresh key per environment.
# RAYSPEC_JWT_SIGNING_KEY="-----BEGIN PRIVATE KEY-----\n...\n-----END PRIVATE KEY-----"

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Reading the boot values from a file instead of the environment
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Each of the three boot values above also accepts a <VAR>_FILE variant naming a
# file to read the value from — a mounted secret (mode 600) the server user can
# read:
#
#   DATABASE_URL_FILE=/run/secrets/database-url
#   RAYSPEC_JWT_SIGNING_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/jwt-signing-key
#   RAYSPEC_API_KEY_PEPPER_FILE=/run/secrets/api-key-pepper
#
# WHY: the value stays out of the image, out of the compose file, out of the
# container's declared environment (so `docker inspect` does not show it), and
# out of the server's own exec environment in /proc/<pid>/environ. It also needs
# no wrapper entrypoint to materialize the secret into the environment first.
# It stays out of the CHILDREN's environment as well: the boot hands the two AUTH
# secrets to the components that need them in-process and never writes them back
# into its environment, so a child process the server spawns is exec'd without
# them and they do not appear in that child's /proc/<pid>/environ either. A value
# supplied as the plain variable instead is left exactly where you put it, and a
# child inherits it like any other environment variable — that is the difference
# the file mount buys.
#
# WHERE: point these OUTSIDE the repository — a tmpfs or orchestrator secret
# mount such as /run/secrets/, or another path only the server user can read. A
# secret file inside a checkout is one `git add -f` away from being committed.
#
# PRECEDENCE: when <VAR>_FILE is set it WINS — the plain variable is not
# consulted at all. An empty / whitespace-only <VAR>_FILE counts as NOT SET (so
# an orchestrator that materializes an unset variable as "" keeps working) and
# the plain variable is used. A <VAR>_FILE left in a local .env therefore takes
# precedence for every component that resolves its configuration from the
# ambient environment.
#
# FAIL-CLOSED: if <VAR>_FILE is set but the file is missing, unreadable, a
# directory, or empty, the server ABORTS. It NEVER falls back to the plain
# variable — a broken secret mount must not silently downgrade to the weaker
# source. The abort names the variable, the path, and — when the read itself
# failed — the OS error code, never the file content.
#
# THE VARIABLE HOLDS A PATH, NOT THE SECRET. The abort messages quote that path
# so the error is actionable, which is a deliberate trade: a secret pasted into
# <VAR>_FILE by mistake is quoted back in the abort. Treat such a value as
# exposed and rotate it.
#
# CONTENT: the file holds the REAL bytes of the value. Surrounding whitespace is
# trimmed, so the trailing newline `echo`/`printf`/a secret projection appends is
# harmless — and for the pepper it must be, since the pepper IS the HMAC key and
# a stray newline would change every api-key hash. This trim is UNIFORM across
# sources: the plain `<VAR>` variable is normalized exactly like a `<VAR>_FILE`
# mount, for all three secrets, so the two forms are byte-equivalent and moving a
# value between them cannot change it. The flip side of the trim: a secret whose
# real bytes begin or end with whitespace cannot be expressed through EITHER form
# and must be base64-encoded.
#
# The trim is harmless but no longer SILENT: whenever it actually changes a
# resolved secret, the boot prints ONE warning naming the variable it came from
# (`<VAR>` or `<VAR>_FILE`) and the kind of change — never the value or any part
# of it. On a key file created with the `>` redirect below that warning is
# EXPECTED and needs no action: the redirect leaves a trailing newline, the trim
# removes it, and the resolved PEM is the same as it always was. The warning
# exists for the other case — a secret that suddenly stops being accepted after
# an upgrade because an invisible character at its edge is now removed. A secret
# with no edge whitespace boots as silently as before. The signing-key file
# holds a REAL multi-line PEM with real newlines — NOT the single-line
# `\n`-escaped form the .env variable above uses. Create it so it is never
# readable by anyone else — the umask in the same subshell as the redirect AND a
# chmod afterwards. The two cover different failure modes: the umask constrains
# a file being CREATED, closing the window a later chmod would leave open, but
# it does not touch a file that already exists — a rotation onto a path an
# earlier key, a `touch`, or a configuration-management copy left group- or
# world-readable keeps that mode when `>` truncates it, and only the chmod
# corrects it.
#
#   (umask 077; openssl genpkey -algorithm RSA -pkeyopt rsa_keygen_bits:2048 \
#     > /run/secrets/jwt-signing-key)
#   chmod 600 /run/secrets/jwt-signing-key
#
# SCOPE: <VAR>_FILE is primarily a SERVER-BOOT mechanism — `rayspec-serve`,
# `rayspec deploy`, and anything else that boots the server read all three
# variants. Among the read-only CLI subcommands, `rayspec plan`'s shadow-apply
# guard (which refuses a migration dry-run that would run against the real
# database by comparing SHADOW_DATABASE_URL to DATABASE_URL) ALSO resolves
# DATABASE_URL_FILE, with precedence over the plain variable — so it still fires
# when the connection string is supplied only through the mount. Because `plan`
# never connects to the real database, a broken DATABASE_URL_FILE there is not
# fatal: it warns once on stderr (naming the variable, path, and OS error code,
# never the content) and the guard simply does not fire. `rayspec dev db` reads
# the plain DATABASE_URL only, so set it as well wherever you run that subcommand.
#
# The local development boot wrapper in examples/local-boot is BOTH. It requires
# all three plain variables of its own accord, because it provisions a throwaway
# dev database from DATABASE_URL before the resolver is ever reached — so a
# _FILE-only environment fails there first, early and loudly. It then points
# DATABASE_URL at that dev database and hands over to the ordinary server boot,
# which resolves from the ambient environment, where <VAR>_FILE still wins (see
# PRECEDENCE above). An ambient DATABASE_URL_FILE therefore outranks the dev
# database the wrapper just provisioned, while the boot banner still names the
# dev database. On a machine that has no such file the boot aborts fail-closed
# instead of retargeting anything silently; leave <VAR>_FILE out of a local .env
# unless you mean it for every boot.

# ── Local `.env` auto-load ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The `.env` this file is copied to is itself read by BOTH `rayspec-serve` and the
# `rayspec` CLI once at startup, and both search the same two paths in the same
# order: the INVOKING directory's `./.env` first and the checkout root's `.env`
# second (per key — so in a vendored/submodule layout the product repo's `./.env`
# wins over one inside the checkout). Each file is loaded only if
# it exists, and a variable the shell or the orchestrator already set is NEVER
# overridden — an explicit value always wins, and a real deployment, where these
# files are absent, is unaffected. Surrounding quotes are stripped and a literal
# `\n` is unescaped, which is what lets the single-line PEM form above work.
#
# Set this to exactly `1` — no other value counts, not even "true" — to skip that
# load entirely and resolve every variable from the ambient environment alone. Useful
# to prove a deployment carries no dependency on a checked-out `.env`.
# RAYSPEC_SKIP_DOTENV=1

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Agent backends (supply the credential for whichever backend your spec uses)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# OpenAI. Serves BOTH the OpenAI backend AND the Pi backend (Pi runs on OpenAI).
OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...
# Optional default model for the OpenAI/Pi backends.
# OPENAI_MODEL=

# Anthropic. The backend accepts EITHER credential, and reads it from THIS server
# process's own environment. The simplest path is your own Anthropic (Claude)
# Console API key (billed per token):
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Subscription OAuth token (from `claude setup-token`) — the subscription path
# instead of the API key. To use it, set ONLY this (leave ANTHROPIC_API_KEY empty)
# and set it HERE, in the server process's environment: the adapter runs each tenant
# under a FRESH CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR that does NOT inherit a machine-level `claude`
# login, and a launcher/harness that scrubs child-process environments will strip
# it — so a working `claude` CLI login on the box is not enough. The SDK precedence
# is ANTHROPIC_API_KEY > CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN: if BOTH are set, the API key wins
# and the API is billed (the subscription token is shadowed), and the boot logs a
# loud warning.
CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=

# Required whenever the Anthropic backend is used: a writable root directory under
# which the adapter creates a per-tenant CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR (credentials + session
# state). Fail-closed at boot if the Anthropic backend is selected and this is unset.
# RAYSPEC_ANTHROPIC_CONFIG_ROOT=/var/lib/rayspec/anthropic

# Opt-in: reuse a machine `claude` login instead of a server-env token. On a box
# where a human has run `claude` login, set this truthy (true | 1 | on) to boot the
# Anthropic backend with NO token/key in the server env. You MUST seed each tenant's
# config dir first — copy the login into ${RAYSPEC_ANTHROPIC_CONFIG_ROOT}/tenant-<id>/
# — and RAYSPEC_ANTHROPIC_CONFIG_ROOT is still required. If a token/key is ALSO set it
# wins over the seeded login (and the boot warns). Unset ⇒ the fail-closed token demand
# above is unchanged. An unsupported value fails closed.
# RAYSPEC_ANTHROPIC_REUSE_LOGIN=false

# Codex backend. Codex authenticates from an on-disk credentials directory (where
# its `auth.json` lives), NOT from an env API key. Point CODEX_HOME at that
# directory. Leave empty to use the default (~/.codex).
CODEX_HOME=
# Optional default model for the Codex backend.
# CODEX_MODEL=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Speech — transcription (audio in) and synthesis (audio out)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Both selectors below are ENV-gated for the handler capabilities: no spec signal declares
# that a HANDLER transcribes or speaks, so leaving one unset simply leaves the `init.stt` /
# `init.tts` capability absent — never a boot error. STT_PROVIDER is separately REQUIRED on
# a product-profile spec that declares an `stt.*` step: that boot fails closed without it.

# STT provider selector and its credential. Setting it also gives every `handler`-kind
# route and every tool handler the optional `init.stt` transcription capability (bytes
# in, transcript out); leaving it unset leaves that capability absent — never a boot error.
# Wired providers: deepgram | fake. `deepgram` demands DEEPGRAM_API_KEY at boot.
# `fake` is a deterministic offline transcriber for dev/CI (no audio is transcribed,
# and the boot warns loudly).
# STT_PROVIDER=deepgram
DEEPGRAM_API_KEY=

# TTS provider selector — the egress half. Setting it gives every `handler`-kind route
# and every tool handler the optional `init.tts` synthesis capability (text in, audio
# bytes out); leaving it unset leaves that capability absent — never a boot error.
# Wired providers: openai | fake. `openai` demands OPENAI_API_KEY at boot — the SAME
# variable the OpenAI and Pi agent backends use, so those deployments need no new
# credential; an Anthropic- or Codex-backed deployment does not carry it and must set it.
# `fake` is a deterministic offline synthesizer for dev/CI (nothing is spoken — every
# call returns the same fixed tone — and the boot warns loudly). It validates `format`
# exactly as the live provider does but encodes nothing, so it always answers WAV bytes
# with an honest `audio/wav` content type whatever container was asked for: read
# `speech.contentType`, never derive it from the requested format.
# TTS_PROVIDER=openai

# Base-URL overrides for the two speech adapters — TEST/DEV SEAMS, both optional and both
# read from the environment at call time (OPENAI_BASE_URL by the OpenAI TTS adapter,
# DEEPGRAM_BASE_URL by the Deepgram transcription adapter). UNSET — the normal case, and
# what a deployment should leave them at — means the adapter calls the provider's real
# host. Set one to point a suite or a dev boot at a local stub/recording proxy instead. A
# trailing slash is stripped either way.
# OPENAI_BASE_URL IS PROCESS-WIDE, not a speech-only knob. The vendored `openai` client
# defaults its base URL to this variable, and the OpenAI agent backend constructs that client
# without passing one — so a boot that points this at a local stub sends that backend's model
# calls to the same stub. Set it only where that is intended (or give the stub a passthrough).
# Two agent backends are NOT redirected by it, for different reasons. Codex deliberately does
# not copy OPENAI_BASE_URL (nor OPENAI_API_KEY / CODEX_API_KEY / CODEX_BASE_URL) into the
# subprocess it launches. Pi is unaffected because its model registry carries an explicit base
# URL per model and passes it to every client it builds, so the client never falls back to the
# variable. So a stub set here does NOT contain Pi: its model calls still go to the real host,
# with the real key.
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com
# DEEPGRAM_BASE_URL=https://api.deepgram.com

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Runtime configuration (optional — sensible defaults apply)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# HTTP listen port.
# PORT=8080

# Boot timeout in milliseconds (default 60000). The server assembles config, connects
# to the database, and applies the migration chain before it starts listening; if that
# step hangs (unreachable database, wrong DATABASE_URL, a stuck migration) the boot
# aborts after this many ms with a diagnostic instead of hanging silently. Raise it only
# if a legitimate boot is slower than the default.
# RAYSPEC_BOOT_TIMEOUT_MS=60000

# Access-token lifetime in SECONDS (default 480 = 8min; blank counts as unset). A
# longer TTL unblocks a long (>8min) recording whose client cannot carry the httpOnly
# refresh cookie, at a cost: the bearer JWT is self-contained and NON-REVOCABLE until
# it expires — the opaque session is the revocation point, but the JWT is not
# re-checked against it on every request — so a longer TTL widens the window in which
# a leaked or stale token stays valid. The ceiling is 86400 (24h); a credential that
# should outlive that is a refresh-flow question, not a TTL bump. Fail-closed and
# never clamped: a value that is not a positive integer, or is above the ceiling,
# ABORTS the boot rather than silently falling back.
# RAYSPEC_ACCESS_TOKEN_TTL_SECONDS=480

# Dev/CI multiplier for the auth rate-limit buckets (default 1 = the production
# limits; blank counts as unset). A positive integer scales the per-minute max of
# exactly the register (5/min), login (10/min) and refresh (30/min) buckets, so a
# test harness that registers more than 5 accounts a minute does not trip the
# register bucket mid-run; the windows and every other bucket are untouched. Any
# value other than 1 makes the boot log a loud one-line warning naming the
# variable and the value — this is a dev/CI posture and must never reach a
# production environment silently. Fail-closed: a value that is not a positive
# integer ABORTS the boot rather than silently falling back.
# RAYSPEC_AUTH_RATE_MULTIPLIER=1

# ── Agent-run bounds (all four OFF unless set) ────────────────────────────────
#
# A provider that accepts a request and never answers keeps a run alive for the
# model client's whole retry window, which on the durable worker occupies one of
# its run slots for that entire time. The first three variables put a ceiling on
# it; the fourth lets a run be ENDED from another process while it waits. Leave
# them unset and runs behave exactly as they did before they existed. An
# unusable value is treated as unset: not a number, or — after flooring — below 1
# or above 2147483647, the largest delay a timer can hold (a bigger one fires
# after 1ms instead of waiting, which would invert the ceiling it configures).
#
# Per-REQUEST timeout in milliseconds for the OpenAI backend's model client.
# Unset ⇒ the openai client's own default, documented as 10 minutes.
# RAYSPEC_AGENT_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=120000
#
# How many ATTEMPTS that client makes for one request — the first try plus its
# retries, so 1 means a single attempt with no retry. Unset ⇒ the openai client's
# own default of 2 retries, i.e. three attempts.
# RAYSPEC_AGENT_MAX_ATTEMPTS=2
#
# Wall-clock ceiling in milliseconds on ONE WHOLE RUN, applied by run-core to both
# the synchronous request path and the durable worker. When it expires run-core
# stops waiting: a synchronous JSON request ends as a 504 GATEWAY_TIMEOUT with the
# neutral `timeout` class (a streaming request gets the terminal error frame with
# that class), while on the durable worker the run's transaction rolls back, so no
# terminal run header is written — a run enqueued through the API keeps the
# `enqueued` header that path writes first, and a cron-fired agent run, enqueued
# without one, leaves no `runs` row at all. It does NOT cancel the model call: the
# ceiling only stops run-core waiting, so the provider request keeps going until
# it settles on its own — the ceiling frees the caller and the worker slot, it
# does not abort the work. (Ending a run and freeing the work is what
# POST /v1/runs/{id}/cancel does, and it is a different thing from this ceiling.)
# run-core refuses what the abandoned call reaches for after that: no event, no
# journal write, and no tool dispatch started once the ceiling has fired (a
# dispatch already in flight runs its handler out, and its journal step is then
# refused).
# RAYSPEC_AGENT_RUN_MAX_MS=600000
#
# How often a run that is EXECUTING re-reads its own cancellation record, in
# milliseconds. OFF by default: without it a cancellation reaches an executing run
# only through the in-process abort signal, so a run on ANOTHER worker process
# keeps burning its provider call until it returns by itself. Set it and the run's
# own process notices the cancellation and ends the run there — the identical
# abort, terminal state and journal as the in-process path.
#
# What it costs: ONE indexed idempotency_keys lookup per IN-FLIGHT run, per
# interval, per worker process. The reads are chained rather than periodic (the
# next one is scheduled after the previous answer), so at most one is ever in
# flight per run and a slow database gets fewer of them, not a backlog. They go
# through the run's autonomous-commit handle — the durable worker's second
# connection, the one the taint marker already uses — never the run's own
# transaction.
#
# What it changes about a cancelled run: the run ENDS where it runs instead of
# burning on. Without this variable it runs to completion and keeps its steps.
# What survives in the journal follows the INVOCATION SHAPE, not which process
# cancelled — a run cancelled in-process leaves exactly the same thing. On the
# durable worker the run executes INSIDE a transaction, so that transaction ROLLS
# BACK, the journal steps it wrote there are discarded, and the run ends with the
# single `cancelled` step. On the synchronous HTTP path there is no transaction,
# so the steps it already committed are KEPT beside the `cancelled` one.
#
# 1000–5000 is a sensible range. Shorter is measurably safe: there is no floor,
# and at the shipped worker sizing (4 concurrent runs over a pool of 5) even a
# 1ms interval left the autonomous taint INSERT at 4ms median / 10ms worst of 20
# samples — an out-of-tree measurement: the repository's pool-saturation test
# covers that slot for the taint INSERT alone, not with this poll running.
# Shortening buys cancellation LATENCY, not more safety — the record is
# authoritative either way, and a run is never re-run because of it.
# RAYSPEC_RUN_CANCEL_POLL_MS=2000

# ── Agent trace export ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Whether agent traces are exported to OpenAI. What that transport carries is run
# metadata and, once an agent calls tools, its TOOL ARGUMENTS and TOOL OUTPUTS —
# the agent SDK strips the model prompt fields before export. The SDK exports by
# DEFAULT; on the `rayspec deploy` path, where the workload being run is most likely
# somebody else's, that export is an affirmative choice instead.
#
# `openai` exports. `off` keeps the traces in the process. Any OTHER value refuses
# the boot by name — a typo must never read as "not `openai`, therefore off".
#
# BOTH `rayspec deploy` AND `rayspec-serve` read it, and both refuse an unusable
# value with the same message. What differs is only what UNSET (or blank) means: on
# `rayspec deploy` it means `off`, and on `rayspec-serve` it leaves the agent SDK's
# own default, which is to EXPORT — so on that entrypoint setting `off` is what
# stops the export. That default is left to the SDK deliberately: a developer
# tracing their own agent is looking at their own run, so only a stated intention
# moves it. The dev-boot wrappers (`examples/local-boot/serve.ts`,
# `deployments/acme-notes/serve.mts`) assemble the server themselves and never read
# this variable at all. The boot banner states the RESOLVED posture
# (`Trace export:`) on every path either way.
#
# NOTE that `rayspec-serve` reads the `.env` this file is copied to as well (see
# "Local `.env` auto-load" above), so uncommenting the line below changes that
# entrypoint too, not just `rayspec deploy`.
# RAYSPEC_AGENT_TRACING=off

# ── Proxy egress ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Nothing here is a RaySpec variable — these are Node's own, listed because a
# deployment whose only route off the box is an HTTP proxy needs all of them.
#
# On Node 22.21.0+ and the whole 24 line and up, Node ignores HTTP_PROXY/HTTPS_PROXY
# unless NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY opts in — and the server honours them on exactly that
# condition: with the opt-in AND at least one non-empty proxy URL it installs a
# proxy-aware dispatcher at boot, so model calls and every other `fetch` in the
# process go through the proxy. Without the opt-in nothing is touched.
# NO_PROXY is honoured — a host named there is still reached directly.
#
# WHAT COUNTS AS THE OPT-IN DIFFERS BY RUNTIME, and the server follows the running
# one rather than picking a single rule. `NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1` works everywhere and
# is what to write. On 22.21.0+ and on 24.5.0+ Node compares it STRICTLY: `true`,
# `0`, `2`, ` 1` and the like are all "off" (measured on 22.21.0, 22.21.1, 24.5.0,
# 25.6.1). On 24.0.0–24.4.x — before the strict rule landed — Node arms on ANY
# non-empty value, `true` and even `0` included (measured on 24.0.0, 24.1.0, 24.2.0,
# 24.3.0, 24.4.0, 24.4.1), and so does the server there. A blank value is "no opt-in"
# on every version.
#
# On OLDER Node (22.0–22.20, and 23.x), NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY does not exist: Node
# ignores the proxy variables unconditionally, and so does the server, which
# installs nothing there. If your deployment needs proxied egress, run Node 22.21.0
# or newer.
# NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY=1
# HTTP_PROXY=http://proxy.internal:3128
# HTTPS_PROXY=http://proxy.internal:3128
# NO_PROXY=localhost,127.0.0.1,.internal

# TCP listen host. LOOPBACK (127.0.0.1) by default — the LOCAL / pre-external-hardening server must
# not be reachable off-box. Set this (e.g. 0.0.0.0 for all interfaces) ONLY as a deliberate opt-in
# once the external-exposure hardening gate is met; the boot banner then logs the ACTUAL bound address.
# RAYSPEC_HOST=127.0.0.1

# Absolute path to the rayspec.yaml spec to deploy at boot. Absent ⇒ auth-only.
# RAYSPEC_SPEC_PATH=/absolute/path/to/rayspec.yaml

# Root directory that every declared handler's `module` path resolves within — the
# path jail for the handler escape hatch, and the root a relative `extensions[]` pack
# directory resolves under. Read only when RAYSPEC_SPEC_PATH is set; unset ⇒ the spec
# file's OWN directory, which is the right answer whenever handlers live beside the
# spec, so set this only when they live elsewhere. Handler module paths are relative
# to the root: an absolute one, or one carrying a `..` segment, is refused.
# RAYSPEC_HANDLER_ROOT=/absolute/path/to/handlers

# CORS: exact allowed browser origins (comma-separated). EMPTY default = no
# cross-origin. Never set a permissive wildcard in production.
# ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://app.example.com

# CORS: extra allowed request headers beyond the base set (comma-separated).
# ALLOWED_REQUEST_HEADERS=

# Frontend security response headers, applied in BOTH boot shapes. A frontend-only
# STATIC PROFILE — a spec declaring only a `frontend` (no stores / api / agents /
# tooling / triggers / handlers / extensions, and no durable worker) — emits them
# on every response, since it runs with no reverse proxy to add them. A normal
# (API-bearing) boot applies them to the responses its declared frontend mounts
# serve — files, the SPA fallback, a mount's 404 page; API/auth responses are
# unchanged and carry no CSP. Each has a secure default when unset; override
# verbatim — the same value means the same thing in both boot shapes.
#
# Content-Security-Policy. Default (no 'unsafe-inline' — a SPA that needs inline
# styles or scripts must set a weaker policy here explicitly):
#   default-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'
# RAYSPEC_FRONTEND_CSP=
#
# Permissions-Policy. Default (deny the high-risk device features):
#   camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()
# RAYSPEC_PERMISSIONS_POLICY=

# Trusted-proxy CIDRs (comma-separated) for rate-limit client-identity. EMPTY default =
# the socket peer is always the identity — no X-Forwarded-For / X-Real-IP header is trusted,
# so a direct caller cannot spoof its throttle identity. Set to your load-balancer / ingress
# hop CIDRs (e.g. 10.0.0.0/8) ONLY when the server sits behind a proxy you control.
# RAYSPEC_TRUSTED_PROXIES=

# Local filesystem root for the blob store (one subdirectory per tenant). Required
# only when the spec declares a binary/stream route.
# RAYSPEC_BLOB_ROOT=/var/lib/rayspec/blobs

# READ-ONLY local filesystem root for the fs-source capability — the jail a tool or
# route handler's `init.fsSource` is confined to, for deployment-static assets
# (reference material, templates, a content directory you placed on the box). No
# route kind requires it, so it is purely a deployment choice: unset ⇒ no fs-source
# is wired at all and a handler that reaches for it fail-closes loudly. Resolved to
# an absolute path, and the root itself checked ONCE — where the fs-source is BUILT,
# which only a boot that DEPLOYS A BACKEND SPEC (a rayspec.yaml declaring stores/routes, or a
# *.product.yaml document) reaches. That boot refuses a root that does not exist or is not a directory, and
# nothing here creates it. The build is gated on this variable ALONE, so a spec
# deploy checks the root whether or not the document declares a handler or tool that
# would receive `init.fsSource`. A boot that deploys no spec never builds the
# fs-source, so it never reaches that check: an AUTH-ONLY boot (no
# RAYSPEC_SPEC_PATH) resolves this value, validates nothing and serves, and a
# frontend-only static boot does not read it at all — a wrong path here therefore
# surfaces on the boot that deploys a spec, not before. SHARED across tenants
# (read-only, deployment-static content — not per-tenant data).
# RAYSPEC_FS_SOURCE_ROOT=/var/lib/rayspec/source

# HS256 secret for signed media-playback tokens — SEPARATE from the JWT key, and
# at least 32 bytes. Required only when the spec declares a playback route.
# RAYSPEC_MEDIA_SIGNING_KEY=change-me-32-bytes-minimum

# ── Media prep (audio products only) ──────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Whether a finished recording is turned into a playable artifact, and which ffmpeg
# does it. The selector below is read ONLY when the deployed product declares audio.
#
# `ffmpeg` (or unset; blank counts as unset) wires the prep step: a finalized track's
# chunks are stitched into ONE playable, seekable artifact and registered, so the
# playback route starts serving real audio instead of the honest 409 not-ready. `off`
# omits the step and playback stays 409. The value is trimmed, so a whitespace-only
# value is blank too; any OTHER non-blank value refuses the boot by name — an invalid
# value is never coerced to the default. Prep itself is fail-soft: a failed remux is
# logged loudly and leaves playback not-ready, but does not poison transcription or
# extraction.
# RAYSPEC_MEDIA_PREP=ffmpeg
#
# The ffmpeg / ffprobe executables, looked up per call. Unset ⇒ `ffmpeg` and
# `ffprobe` as found on PATH; set them for a non-PATH install. They are also on the
# TRANSCRIPTION path — a track's chunks are stitched before they reach the
# speech-to-text provider — so an audio product that transcribes needs them even with
# the selector `off`.
# RAYSPEC_FFMPEG_BIN=/usr/bin/ffmpeg
# RAYSPEC_FFPROBE_BIN=/usr/bin/ffprobe
#
# Wall-clock cap in milliseconds on ONE ffmpeg or ffprobe child (default 120000 =
# 120s, generous for what is a container rewrite rather than a re-encode). A hanging
# child would otherwise stall the durable worker's step indefinitely; on expiry the
# child is SIGKILLed and the step ends as a fail-closed remux error, never an
# indefinite wait. An unusable value uses the default: not a number, or — after
# flooring — below 1 or above 2147483647, the largest delay a timer can hold (a
# bigger one fires after 1ms instead of waiting, which would invert the cap it
# configures and kill a healthy child within milliseconds).
# RAYSPEC_FFMPEG_TIMEOUT_MS=120000

# The single deployment tenant (org id) every product-profile deploy binds to
# (single-node posture). Required by EVERY product deploy. It must be a UUID AND
# name an org that exists and is not soft-deleted: the boot REFUSES to start
# otherwise, because every principal of the deployment is bound to this tenant, so
# a phantom one serves nobody. Since `deploy` also serves the auth surface, a
# product deployment cannot create its own org — create it first, deploy second.
# In production that is ONE step, against this same DATABASE_URL, no server and no
# HTTP route involved (it also applies the committed migration chain, so point it
# only at a database you mean to migrate):
#   1. rayspec tenant ensure --org-id <the uuid> --name <n>
#        [--owner-email <e> --owner-invite-out <path>]   (the owner handoff; the
#        token lands in that mode-600 file and is printed nowhere)
#   2. deploy with RAYSPEC_PRODUCT_TENANT_ID set to that uuid.
# It is idempotent — re-running with the same id resolves the same org.
# Locally you can instead go through a running server:
#   1. RAYSPEC_TENANT_BOOTSTRAP_ENABLED=true rayspec-serve      (auth only, no spec)
#   2. rayspec dev bootstrap-tenant --base-url <url> --org-id <the uuid>
#   3. deploy with RAYSPEC_PRODUCT_TENANT_ID set to that uuid, the gate off again.
# Against an org that already exists, skip straight to the deploy with its id.
# RAYSPEC_PRODUCT_TENANT_ID=<org-uuid>

# Operator gate for the tenant bootstrap. Enabled ONLY when set to the exact string
# "true"; anything else leaves POST /v1/auth/bootstrap-tenant UNREGISTERED (404).
# It is the only route on which an org id may be chosen by the caller — the public
# POST /v1/auth/register never accepts one, because someone who learned the id you
# intend to deploy against could otherwise create that org first and own it. Turn
# it on for the local bootstrap boot, off for the deployment. `rayspec tenant
# ensure` does NOT need it — it reaches the database directly — so a production
# deployment can leave this unset forever and never register that route at all.
# RAYSPEC_TENANT_BOOTSTRAP_ENABLED=false

# Extraction executor mode — required when the deployed product declares agents:
#   live          = real agent runs (runAgent / gpt-5)
#   deterministic = injected executor (dev/CI only)
# RAYSPEC_EXTRACTION_MODE=live

# Single-file override for the extraction config path. A product declaring ONE
# extractor resolves its config from `extraction/<extractor_id>.extractor.json`
# beside the spec if that file exists, else from `extraction/extractor.json`; point
# this at a file named something else instead. Leave it UNSET for a conventionally
# named config — setting it there is redundant and overrides the resolution you want.
# A product declaring MORE THAN ONE extractor refuses the boot when this is set at
# all, because one file cannot say which extractor it configures; give those a
# per-extractor file.
# RAYSPEC_EXTRACTION_CONFIG=/absolute/path/to/extractor.json

# Conversation reply executor — required when the deployed product declares a
# conversation input:
#   live          = real agent runs through the configured backend
#   deterministic = an injected reply backend (dev/CI only)
# The platform ships NO deterministic backend, so `deterministic` refuses the boot
# unless an embedder injected one; any other value refuses it by name. A boot that
# does select it prints a loud NON-REAL PROVIDER(S) SELECTED banner naming this
# variable, beside the same warning for a fake STT_PROVIDER or a deterministic
# RAYSPEC_EXTRACTION_MODE, and calls it a dev/CI posture — not fail-closed, because a
# dev boot legitimately uses it.
# RAYSPEC_RESPONDER_MODE=live

# Record input-normalize executor — the same two values, the same refusals and the
# same banner, required when the deployed product's record input declares an
# input-normalize step. A record input without one needs neither this nor a
# normalizer config.
# RAYSPEC_NORMALIZE_MODE=live

# The tenant (org id) that the deployment's cron triggers fire under. Required
# only when the spec declares cron triggers on a durable worker. The org does NOT
# have to exist at boot: the deployment comes up, skips every firing with one log
# line while it is missing, and starts firing by itself the moment an `orgs` row
# with THIS id exists (no restart). The id has to MATCH that row — the org id is
# assigned when the row is created, and `POST /v1/orgs`, `POST /v1/auth/register`
# and a plain `rayspec dev bootstrap-tenant` all let the database generate it, so
# with those you read the id back from the response and set it here. To settle the
# id up front instead, create the `orgs` row with that id — either by hand, or with
# `rayspec dev bootstrap-tenant --org-id <uuid>` against a server booted with
# RAYSPEC_TENANT_BOOTSTRAP_ENABLED=true (see above). A malformed (non-UUID) value
# still refuses the boot, as does leaving this unset.
# RAYSPEC_CRON_TENANT_ID=

# Operator gate for irreversible tenant data erasure. Enabled ONLY when set to the
# exact string "true"; anything else keeps erasure in dry-run (zero deletes).
# RAYSPEC_ERASURE_ENABLED=false

# Operator gate that lets a NON-browser client receive the rotated refresh secret in
# the JSON response body, so a desktop or CLI client can hold it in OS-secure storage
# rather than the httpOnly cookie. Enabled ONLY when set to the exact string "true";
# anything else ignores the client's opt-in and the secret rides the cookie alone.
# RAYSPEC_BODY_REFRESH_ENABLED=false

# ── Daily system cleanup (durable-worker deployments only) ────────────────────
#
# ONE platform-wide housekeeping job across all tenants: it hard-deletes EXPIRED OIDC
# token rows (always live, no gate) and runs the operator-gated GDPR tombstone purge.
# It rides the durable worker: every *.product.yaml boot launches one, a classic
# rayspec.yaml launches one only when it declares `deployment.durableWorker: true`,
# and an auth-only boot never does — so nothing here runs on a deployment without a
# worker and eligible rows accumulate there until a worker boot. It is not a tenant
# cron trigger and does not use RAYSPEC_CRON_TENANT_ID.
#
# The crontab it fires on. Unset or blank ⇒ `0 3 * * *` (03:00 daily, a quiet hour).
# The boot validates the expression through the scheduler's own parser: the scheduler
# takes the standard 5-field crontab and the 6-field form that prepends a seconds
# field, and a valid expression reaches it exactly as written here. An expression it
# cannot parse (shorthand such as `@daily`, a 4-field expression, an out-of-range
# field) aborts the boot with `Boot aborted — RAYSPEC_CLEANUP_SCHEDULE='<value>' is
# not a crontab the scheduler can parse (<the parser's own detail>)` — and it does so
# on any boot that reads this variable, not only one that wires a durable worker. A
# frontend-only static boot does not read it at all — it branches to the static
# profile before the environment is resolved — so an unparseable value there is
# served, not refused.
# RAYSPEC_CLEANUP_SCHEDULE="0 3 * * *"
#
# Operator gate for the irreversible GDPR tombstone purge. Enabled ONLY when set to
# the exact string "true"; anything else keeps the purge in dry-run — it COUNTS what
# it would delete, so the backlog stays visible, and deletes nothing.
# RAYSPEC_GDPR_PURGE_ENABLED=false
#
# Retention window in DAYS before a soft-deleted (tombstoned) row becomes eligible
# for that purge (default 30; blank counts as unset). A user tombstone always uses
# this value; a membership tombstone uses its own org's `retention_days` when that is
# set, else this. Fail-closed: a value that is not a number, or is negative, ABORTS
# the boot rather than falling back, because a wrong window silently over- or
# under-retains personal data.
# RAYSPEC_GDPR_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# ── Schema update (unset unless you are applying a reviewed delta) ────────────
#
# Path to the reviewed forward-DELTA `.sql` that evolves an ALREADY materialized
# schema in place, so existing rows survive. Setting it puts the boot in update mode:
# the deploy engine SCANS the delta and BLOCKS any destructive statement no reviewed
# allowlist entry covers, then applies it. `rayspec deploy --apply-migration <delta>`
# sets this for you; setting it in the deployment environment is the equivalent. An
# unreadable path refuses the boot. The scan is deliberately NARROW, not total:
# nothing destructive passes unreviewed, but additive DDL and data statements flow
# through by design (and DELETE-with-WHERE is flagged while UPDATE-with-WHERE is
# not), because a delta named in the deployment environment is trusted at the same
# level as the spec path itself.
#
# It is safe across a restart: the boot classifies the live schema FIRST, so a schema
# that already satisfies the new spec MOUNTS with a loud log instead of re-applying a
# non-idempotent delta. Pointed at a schema that was never materialized it refuses
# and tells you to boot once without it. Still, drop it once the delta has landed, so
# the operator intent stays explicit.
# RAYSPEC_UPDATE_MIGRATION=/absolute/path/to/0001_delta.sql
#
# The reviewed destructive-statement allowlist for that delta — a JSON array whose
# entries each carry a `kind`, a `match` and a `reason`. Optional: a purely additive
# delta needs none, and unset means an EMPTY allowlist, never a permissive one.
# Fail-closed: a path that is unreadable, not valid JSON, not an array, or that holds
# an entry missing any of the three fields refuses the boot rather than proceeding
# with an empty allowlist.
# RAYSPEC_UPDATE_ALLOWLIST=/absolute/path/to/allowlist.json
